course: reflection
lesson: 23
title: When nobody sits with you: making friends again
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Loneliness can become a circle: three months into a new school, Zainab still ate lunch alone most days, and at night she thought: "Back home I had loads of mates. Here I'm invisible. Maybe I'm just not likeable any more."
explain: The first weeks she was nervous but hopeful; by month two she felt flat and tired and stopped trying to join in, and pulling back made her even more alone, so the circle turned again; hiding in the library every lunch meant nobody even saw she needed someone.
explain: Then a teacher noticed and found her a lunchtime art club; when someone asked her to sit with them, she said yes; by month four she had two friends; she was never unlikeable, her friendships just hadn't had time or a place to grow.
explain: Other kids feel like the odd one out for reasons nobody can see: a dad away on an oil rig, a family that keeps Eid not Christmas, a home where Mum is very ill and you help out; sharing a bit of your story often turns "weird" into "interesting".
explain: When friends whisper and stop as you walk over, the worried brain fills the gap with the worst story; sometimes, like for Ella, the whisper was a birthday surprise, so asking beats assuming.
fact: Loneliness can make people pull back, and pulling back makes loneliness worse; this circle can be broken by small steps like a club or saying yes to an invitation.
fact: Feeling lonely at a new school does not mean you are unlikeable; friendships need time and places to grow.
fact: Teachers often notice lonely pupils and can help with clubs and buddies; telling one speeds this up.
fact: Some parents work away for weeks, for example on oil rigs at sea; missing them is a sign of love, and video calls and special visits help.
fact: Families celebrate different festivals, such as Eid (Muslim), Diwali (Hindu, Sikh and Jain) and Hanukkah (Jewish); sharing your own can help others learn and help you feel seen.
fact: Some families do not celebrate birthdays for religious reasons; explaining calmly helps most friends understand.
fact: New players often need time before a team trusts them; "like a lemon" is UK slang for looking silly or useless.
fact: When we are worried, the brain fills gaps with the worst story; "paranoid" means wrongly sure people are against you.
fact: Home-schooled children can find friends through clubs, sports and activities.
fact: Swimming can be learned at any age, and many children learn later than their classmates.
fact: A young carer is a child who helps look after an ill or disabled family member; young carers' groups exist, and friends can visit instead of always being visited.
fact: When a best friend moves away, it leaves a gap; new friends usually fill it slowly, often through group games and clubs.
exercise: Zainab ate alone for three months and stopped trying. Why did it get worse? | Pulling back made her more alone, like a circle | Everyone at the school had agreed to ignore her | She had become less likeable since moving | The other kids were not allowed to talk to new pupils
exercise: What does Zainab's story show about being "not likeable any more"? | It wasn't true; friendships just hadn't had time to grow | It was true; moving changes who people like | It was true until she changed her whole personality to fit in better | It's true for everyone after age nine
exercise: What did NOT help Zainab? | Hiding in the library every lunch | The teacher noticing her | The lunchtime art club | Saying yes to sitting with someone
exercise: Ben's dad works on an oil rig and missed sports day. What helped? | Dad watching the race on video and calling straight after | Saying "I don't care" to everyone who asked | Refusing to run because Dad wasn't there | Pretending to everyone that his dad was there somewhere in the crowd
exercise: What does missing his dad show? | That Ben loves him | That Ben is being babyish | That his dad doesn't care | That something is wrong with Ben
exercise: Mia's family celebrates Eid, not Christmas, and she feels odd in December. What helped? | Telling the class about Eid | Pretending she celebrated Christmas | Staying home all of December | Asking her family to switch festivals
exercise: Kian is new on the football team and nobody passes to him. What helped? | The coach mixing up training pairs | Quitting after two weeks | Taking the ball himself and never passing to anyone | Shouting at the other players
exercise: Ella's friends whisper and stop when she comes over. What was really going on? | They were planning her birthday surprise | They were planning to dump her as a friend | They were saying bad things about her family | They were copying each other's homework
exercise: What lesson did Ella take from it? | Ask instead of assuming | Never trust friends who whisper | Always whisper back about them | Expect a surprise party every time
exercise: Noah is home-schooled and has no friends his age. What helped? | Joining a sports club | Waiting for friends to find him | Playing only online with strangers | Asking to go to school at night
exercise: Sophie's family doesn't celebrate birthdays for religious reasons. What helped most? | Explaining her family's traditions to friends | Hiding it and pretending she had parties | Asking the school to ban all birthday cake for everybody in the class | Eating the cake secretly when nobody looks
exercise: Lucas can't swim and sat out at the pool party. What helped? | Swimming lessons; he swam a width after a term | Avoiding every pool for the rest of his life so nobody would know | Saying he had an ear infection every time | Jumping in the deep end to learn quickly
exercise: Harry can't go to friends' houses because he helps his very ill mum. What is he? | A young carer | A babysitter | A home-schooler | A nurse
exercise: What helped Harry? | Friends visiting him at home and a young carers' group | Hiding the real reason from everyone so nobody felt sorry for him | Leaving his mum alone so he could go out | Telling friends he just didn't like them
exercise: Grace's best friend moved away. What helped her? | Joining a group game | Waiting for someone to ask her | Spending every break alone | Calling her old friend every break
test: Zainab's cousin from London goes, "you're not invisible, you're just new, it's bare normal." ("Bare" in London slang means very.) What's the true point? | Feeling invisible at a new school is common and passes | Zainab really is invisible and nobody sees her | Being new means you'll be alone all year | London kids never feel lonely at new schools
test: What would happen if Zainab kept hiding in the library every lunch? | The loneliness circle would probably keep turning | Friends would come and find her in the library | She would suddenly become popular | It would make no difference either way to how lonely she felt
test: Ben tells everyone, "I don't care that my dad's not here." Is that true? | Probably not; it hides how much he misses him | Yes; Ben really doesn't care about his dad being away at all | Yes; boys don't miss their dads | No; he is angry at the other dads
test: A kid asks Mia, "wait, you don't do Christmas? that's so weird." What's a good response? | Tell him about Eid | Agree it's weird and pretend to do Christmas | Say his festivals are weird instead | Walk off and never talk about it again
test: Kian's coach from Manchester says, "You looked like a right lemon out there, but they'll pass when they know you." ("Like a lemon" means looking silly.) What's the point? | New players need time before the team trusts them | Kian should quit because he looks silly | Kian should stop passing the ball to them either, to get them back | Lemons are bad at football
test: Ella lies awake: "They're talking about me. They're gonna ditch me." What's the brain doing? | Filling the gap with the worst story because she's worried | Reading her friends' minds correctly and seeing what they really think | Predicting the future accurately | Warning her about a real danger
test: Noah's mum, from Pittsburgh, says, "Yinz kids'll find your crowd at the rec centre." ("Yinz" means you all, in Pittsburgh.) What's her advice? | Join clubs and activities | Never leave home without her | Only make friends with cousins | Home-schooled kids can't make friends
test: Sophie's mate says, "no birthdays? that's lowkey sad." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What helps both of them? | Sophie explaining her family's traditions | Sophie pretending to have birthdays | Her mate telling everyone it's weird | Avoiding the topic of birthdays forever, so nobody feels awkward
test: Lucas thinks, "I'm sat here like a granny while everyone splashes." What's the true thing? | Swimming can be learned at any age | Only grannies sit out at pool parties | If you can't swim at nine, you never will | He should jump in to prove himself
test: Harry's teacher says, "He's a young carer." What does Harry's mate now understand? | Harry helps look after his ill mum, so friends can visit him | Harry is a nurse at the hospital after school | Harry doesn't want to be friends with anybody | Harry is too busy because of homework
test: In a cartoon, the new kid gets a best friend in the first five minutes. What's more real? | Friendships often take weeks or months | Real new kids always find friends in five minutes | Real new kids never make any friends | Friends only come if you're the best at something
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between loneliness and withdrawal? | Withdrawal tends to aggravate loneliness, forming a self-reinforcing circle | Withdrawal invariably remedies loneliness within a week | Loneliness and withdrawal are wholly unconnected and arise independently | Loneliness arises solely in persons who are unlikeable
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a worried pupil may conclude that whispering friends are speaking ill of her. | The anxious mind supplies the worst explanation where facts are absent | Whispering is invariably employed to convey malicious remarks | Friends are obliged by custom to disclose all conversations | Pupils possess an accurate capacity to perceive others' thoughts
exam: It is given that a child assists in the care of a seriously ill parent. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The child is termed a young carer, and support groups exist for such children | The child is prohibited from maintaining any friendships | The child is required by law to conceal the circumstances from all other persons | Such a child is employed by the health service as a nurse
exam: In respect of the acquisition of swimming ability, determine which statement is correct. | Swimming may be learned at any age with instruction | Swimming cannot be learned after the age of seven | Swimming is acquired solely by entering deep water unaided | Persons unable to swim at nine shall remain so permanently
